      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. The dose looks unsafe, and I'd like it confirmed first.\n\n- **Dose:** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. The usual maximum for adults is 2,550 mg/day (up to 3,000 mg/day in some references). Standard single doses are 500\u20131,000 mg, and 1700 mg in one dose is also above that range. This much metformin raises the risk of serious lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n- **Prescriber:** You've said you're not a clinician. Medication orders normally need to come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one.\n\n**Next steps:**\n1. Ask the prescribing clinician to confirm the intended dose. It may be a typo. For example, 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 1000 mg twice daily would both be within limits.\n2. Have the prescriber give me the corrected order, or confirm that they really mean 1700 mg three times daily.\n\nOnce I have a confirmed, safe dose, I'll enter it right away."
      }
